{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"DevOps Agent IAM permissions","context":"# DevOps Agent IAM permissions\n\nAWS DevOps Agent uses service-specific AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) actions to control access to its features and capabilities. These actions determine what users can do within the AWS DevOps Agent console and Operator Web App. This is separate from the AWS service API permissions that the agent itself uses to investigate your resources.\n\nFor more information about limiting agent access, see Limiting Agent Access in an AWS Account.\n\nTo let the agent perform directed actions in your AWS accounts, register an elevated IAM role on the account association. For example, you might use an elevated role to remediate an issue after operator approval. For more information about directed actions and registering an elevated IAM role, see Working with directed actions","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/devopsagent/latest/userguide/aws-devops-agent-security-devops-agent-iam-permissions.html"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Tracing failures across Terraform state boundaries with AWS DevOps Agent | AWS DevOps & Developer Productivity Blog","context":"## Assessment and key takeaways\n\nBeing honest about what the agent does well and where it has limits matters more than a clean demo.\n\n**What it got right:**\n\n* The agent identified networking as the root cause instead of chasing DynamoDB or IAM, which is exactly where the human investigation stalls.\n* It traced the VPC endpoint connectivity path accurately and correlated the two alarms into a single event.\n* The DynamoDB red herring didn’t fool it, because it explained the capacity drop as a downstream symptom.\n* CloudTrail analysis surfaced the security group change, and the agent tied its timestamp to the onset of failures.\n* Most importantly, it turned a cross-team investigation that spans multiple stacks into a single, correlated result.\n\n**Where to keep expectations realistic:**\n\n* The agent reasons about AWS resources and their relationships, not Terraform stacks. That’s a strength here, because it sees past the state boundary. It does mean the agent won’t tell you “this was in the networking stack.” Instead, it names the security group and the change, and you map that back to the stack.\n* When a security group change is made by running `terraform apply`, CloudTrail records the IAM role that ran the apply, not the individual engineer. Attribution stops at the automation identity unless you have additional context.\n* The quality of the network-layer diagnosis depends on signals being available. With VPC Flow Logs active in this demo, the agent gains concrete evidence. Without them, it has less to work with and may flag the gap.\n\nThe broader takeaway is that Terraform state separation is the right call for team ownership, but it manufactures operational visibility gaps. The team that owns the symptoms often doesn’t own the cause and can’t see the change that triggered it. AWS DevOps Agent helps address that gap by understanding resource topology regardless of how you provisioned the resources or which state file describes them","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/devops/tracing-failures-across-terraform-state-boundaries-with-aws-devops-agent/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"Permissions for AWS DevOps Agent","context":"# Permissions for AWS DevOps Agent\n\nIf you set up AWS DevOps Agent for the **Operate** capabilities, your identity needs two kinds of permissions: read access so the IDE extension can show status, and permissions to create the access role and Agent Space.\n\nTo show the Agent Space status and its integrations, the IDE extension uses AWS DevOps Agent read permissions, including `aidevops:ListServices` and `aidevops:ListAssociations`.\n\nSetting up AWS DevOps Agent requires permission to create an IAM role and an Agent Space. Your coding agent checks your permissions before setup. If anything is missing, it tells you what’s needed and gives you a note to send to your AWS administrator. For the permissions AWS DevOps Agent uses, see DevOps Agent IAM permissions in the *AWS DevOps Agent User Guide*.\n\nThe access role trusts only the Agent Space in your account. Setup does not attach `AIDevOpsAgentActionsPolicy` and does not create an elevated role, so AWS DevOps Agent recommends fixes but does not apply them. AWS DevOps Agent actions are logged in AWS CloudTrail. To remove AWS DevOps Agent, delete the access role or the Agent Space. For more information about how AWS DevOps Agent uses IAM roles and Agent Spaces, see the AWS DevOps Agent User Guide","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/startupadvisor/latest/userguide/security-iam-devops-agent.html"}]}}

